  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nToday the local area is under an influence of a upper level ridge \ncentered over the southern Plains and lower Mississippi River \nValley. This will bring warm temperatures to most of the area south \nof a weak surface cold front stretched across northern Missouri. \nSouth of this front temperatures will achieve the low to mid 90s \nwith heat index values in the 100-105 range. For areas behind the \nfront across the northern CWA temperatures will hold in the low to \nmid 80s. Tonight, the boundary will become stationary near the \nHighway 36 corridor as a LLJ gets ramped up and noses into the area. \nThis will bring the chance for a few storms across the north central \nand northeastern Missouri overnight into tomorrow morning. Tomorrow, \nthe upper level ridge become amplified over the region as the \nstationary front lifts north of the area as a warm front. Increased \nWAA will aid in highs rising into the upper 80s to upper 90s. Heat \nindex values will range from the upper 90s to near 105. \n\nWe are looking at the potential for an active weekend ahead. There \nis increased confidence that the upper-level trough in the Pacific \nNW will progress southeast Saturday night, beginning to flatten out \nthe ridge of high pressure over the SE CONUS. The exit region of the \ntrough will likely set up over the rockies, favoring the development \nof a surface low and synoptic cold front at the surface. There is \nstill some uncertainty about where this front will set up Friday \nnight but the current ensemble consensus places the front along NW \nMO and extending from the CO foothills into central IA. This \nboundary may coincide with the development of a nocturnal LLJ Friday \nnight. While we are still far enough out that the CAMs can't resolve \nthe entire event Friday night into Saturday, there is a signal for \nfairly strong CAPE values Friday evening over NW MO. All these \nmechanisms favor ascent and we could see the potential for severe \nweather, primarily damaging winds, for northern MO Friday night. \n\nStorms will potentially stick around throughout the weekend as \nensemble consensus shows the front stalling out across our CWA as \nthe trough and ridge fight for dominance over our CWA. This setup \nfavors the development of shortwaves that could bring several rounds \nof showers Saturday and Sunday. A few of the storms on Saturday\nafternoon/evening may be strong to severe with isolated damaging\nwinds the main threat. Deterministic guidance also has PWAT \nvalues near 2.0 inches along the front, which is above average \nfor this time of year and as a result we could see locally heavy\nrainfall along the front. The air behind the cold front looks \nto be considerably cooler so we can't rule out the potential for\nconvection and storms on Sunday but we will have a better idea \nabout this potential once we are in range for higher resolution \nguidance. \n\nOn the bright side, there is high confidence that the cold front \nwill move through our area Monday with ensemble guidance (25th-75th \npercentile) bringing highs down into the 70s for most of the CWA and \nlow 80s for our southern most counties. Guidance also shows the \nupper-level ridge weakening after the frontal passage resulting in \nmore zonal flow that could keep rain chances around Monday and \nTuesday.",
